//? Title: Check chain agreement and sampling efficiency
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

> Distinguish chain disagreement from slow sampling

Rank-normalized split R-hat compares the location of the chains. Ranking
absolute deviations from the pooled median gives the folded version, which
can detect scale differences even when the chains have similar centers.
The reported R-hat is the larger of the two components.

Bulk effective sample size (ESS) estimates sampling efficiency for the
rank-normalized draws. The lower and upper ESS use indicators for the
pooled 5th and 95th percentiles. The smaller tail ESS identifies the less
efficient tail in the two-quantile summary.

```{cpp, id=rank-chain-diagnostics, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/bayes/diagnostics.hpp>
#include <datamunge/random/random.hpp>
#include <cmath>
#include <print>

int main() {
    using namespace datamunge;
    random::SplitMix64 rng(42);
    constexpr std::size_t draws = 512;
    constexpr double persistence = 0.95;
    const double innovation_scale = std::sqrt(1 - persistence * persistence);
    std::vector<std::vector<double>> independent(4), autocorrelated(4);
    for (std::size_t chain = 0; chain < independent.size(); ++chain) {
        double state = rng.normal();
        for (std::size_t draw = 0; draw < draws; ++draw) {
            const double innovation = rng.normal();
            state = persistence * state + innovation_scale * innovation;
            independent[chain].push_back(innovation);
            autocorrelated[chain].push_back(state);
        }
    }
    auto shifted = independent;
    auto wider = independent;
    for (auto& draw : shifted.back())
        draw += 2;
    for (auto& draw : wider.back())
        draw *= 4;

    const auto show = [](const char* label, const auto& chains) {
        const auto rhat = bayes::rank_normalized_rhat(chains);
        const auto ess = bayes::bulk_tail_effective_sample_size(chains);
        std::println("| {} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.0f} | {:.0f} | {:.0f} |", label,
                     rhat.rank_normalized, rhat.folded, ess.bulk, ess.lower_tail, ess.upper_tail);
    };
    std::println("| Chains | Rank R-hat | Folded R-hat | Bulk ESS | Lower ESS | Upper ESS |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    show("Independent standard normals", independent);
    show("One chain shifted", shifted);
    show("One chain wider", wider);
    show("Autocorrelated standard normals", autocorrelated);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Chains | Rank R-hat | Folded R-hat | Bulk ESS | Lower ESS | Upper ESS |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Independent standard normals | 1.000 | 1.002 | 2095 | 2205 | 2081 |
| One chain shifted | 1.323 | 1.100 | 10 | 1973 | 27 |
| One chain wider | 1.000 | 1.199 | 2056 | 35 | 27 |
| Autocorrelated standard normals | 1.066 | 1.004 | 50 | 147 | 236 |
// result_end

The shifted chain mainly raises rank R-hat. The wider chain mainly raises
folded R-hat and lowers tail ESS. Autocorrelation lowers ESS even though
each chain has the same marginal distribution. ESS can exceed the 2,048
retained draws when the estimated autocorrelation time is below one.

[Vehtari et al. (2021), Rank-normalization, folding, and localization|https://doi.org/10.1214/20-BA1221]
